  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 330 AM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...An unsettled weather pattern with cooler and wetter \nconditions is expected this weekend.\n\nA cold front is currently dropping south through the forecast area \nthis morning. Winds have shifted to the north behind the front and \nareas in southern VA and NE NC will see a similar wind shift as the \nfront moves through over the next couple of hours. There are a \nfew showers well behind the front over the Northern Neck and \nEastern Shore, but coverage is quite low due to relatively \nabsent forcing aloft. There is a larger batch of rain riding ENE\nalong the I-81 and I-95 corridors N of our CWA. Expect this \nprecip to gradually drop into our area later this morning and \nafternoon, with the highest coverage generally N of a Farmville-\nPetersburg- Melfa line. There is a good chance areas in \nsouthern VA and NE NC stay mostly dry today. Temperatures today \nwill be a far cry from yesterday and may struggle to increase \nthis afternoon due to modest cool advection and the \nclouds/precip. Highs range from the low-mid 60s N to lower-mid \n70s S. \n\nA second cold front will approach the area Sunday. Tropical moisture \nstreaming up between the fronts will bring additional rain to the \narea late Sat night through Sunday. The initial batch of widespread \nprecip likely moves in from the SW after midnight Saturday into \nearly Sunday morning, with categorical (80%) PoPs spreading E/NE \nthrough the day Sunday. Precip will gradually clear from NW to SE, \nfirst across the W/NW Sunday afternoon and then for the E/SE by \nSunday night. The QPF forecast remains a bit tricky, with some \nemerging discrepancies between the hi-res CAMs and the global models \nand ensembles. For example, the HRRR and NAMNest both have a minima \nin QPF across SE VA and NE NC, whereas the EPS, GEFS, and NBM \ncontinue to depict the highest totals in these areas. At this point, \nthe QPF forecast shows a bifurcation with an area of higher totals \nfrom the nrn Piedmont into the Northern Neck and another area in far \nSE VA and NE NC. Totals up to 1.5\" are generally possible, with \nlocally higher amounts in the above-mentioned areas. \nRegardless, there are likely to be some areas that don't see \nmuch rain owing to difficult-to-predict mesoscale features. The \nchance for thunderstorms remains quite low due to cool sfc \ntemps, but a brief increase in CAPE across the far SE Sunday \nafternoon could allow for an embedded storm or two. With clouds \nand rain around, am again expecting highs to be a bit below \nseasonal norms and ranging from the mid 60s NW to mid 70s SE. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Seasonable temperatures and dry next week.  \n\nA strong area of cool high pressure (1020-1025mb) builds in behind \nthe second front while subtle troughing sets up aloft. High pressure \nremains in control for the bulk of the week, leading to a nice fall \nweek. Highs will stay in the mid 60s to mid 70s through the week, \nwhich is around the average for this time of year. Chilly overnight \ntemperatures are expected as well, especially for the piedmont where \ntemps in the 40s are expected all week. Statistical guidance even \nsuggests upper 30s in the piedmont for the Wed morning low.",
